1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,856,15170,59,839,62,17,220,23,27275,59,2533,12549,1124,11520,839,62,17,220,23,284,220,18,59,701,582,614,510,59,9640,59,839,62,23,856,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,856,15170,18,92,284,1124,37018,90,88,15170,18,27275,59,2533,3136,3696,10607,1124,11520,839,62,23,856,284,1124,37018,90,88,15170,18,11035,8,1182,1119,279,23606,11,582,633,510,59,9640,59,839,62,17,1124,2359,7,1124,37018,90,88,15170,18,92,1124,1291,8,284,1124,839,62,23,379,624,59,2533,7039,11,582,1184,311,3158,1124,11520,839,62,23,379,57758,304,3793,315,2331,220,17,89936,1011,13,12091,279,2297,315,2331,14806,11,582,614,510,59,9640,59,839,62,23,379,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,379,15170,59,839,62,17,220,23,92,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,379,15170,18,27275,59,2533,3136,3696,10607,1124,11520,839,62,23,379,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,379,15170,18,11035,8,1119,279,23606,11,582,633,510,59,9640,59,839,62,17,1124,2359,7,1124,37018,90,88,15170,18,92,1124,1291,8,284,1124,37018,35702,839,62,17,379,15170,18,27275,59,21675,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind $(\\log_2 x)^2$ if $\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the problem, we start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's introduce a substitution to simplify the equation. Let:\n\\[\ny = \\log_2 x.\n\\]\n\nUsing this substitution, we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to express \\(\\log_8 x\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Recall that:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{\\log_2 8}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_2 8 = 3\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} = \\frac{y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 x = \\frac{y}{3}\\) back into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\log_8 y.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to express \\(\\log_8 y\\) in terms of base 2 logarithms. Using the change of base formula, we have:\n\\[\n\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{\\log_2 8} = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\log_8 y = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"797f2a205567e2bb75ea82d01e1cd729cb6e306d8fb1ce760f39c66d314a2b1f"},"seconds":23.52747941017151,"seed":168571000379642811,"stop":"eos","text":"We now have:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, we can rewrite the left side:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nTo clear the fraction, we multiply every term by 3:\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - 3 \\log_2 3 = \\log_2 y.\n\\]\n\nRearranging the equation, we get:\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\log_2 y\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(y = \\log_2 x\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nTo find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\), we need to square \\(y = \\log_2 x\\):\n\\[\n(\\log_2 x)^2 = y^2 = \\left(2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}}\\right)^2 = 2^{3 \\log_2 3} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^3 = 3^3 = 27.\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{27}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"We now have:\n\\[\n\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{y}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, we can rewrite the left side:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 y}{3}.\n\\]\n\nTo clear the fraction, we multiply every term by 3:\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - 3 \\log_2 3 = \\log_2 y.\n\\]\n\nRearranging the equation, we get:\n\\[\n3 \\log_2 y - \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\log_2 y = 3 \\log_2 3.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\log_2 y\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2 y = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(y = \\log_2 x\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\log_2 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}.\n\\]\n\nTo find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\), we need to square \\(y = \\log_2 x\\):\n\\[\n(\\log_2 x)^2 = y^2 = \\left(2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}}\\right)^2 = 2^{3 \\log_2 3} = (2^{\\log_2 3})^3 = 3^3 = 27.\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}.\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:434"}
